[[:db/retract 17592186127171 :answer-field/choices 17592186127172] [:db/retract 17592186127171 :answer-field/choices 17592186127173] [:db/retract 17592186127171 :answer-field/choices 17592186127174] [:db/retract 17592186127171 :answer-field/choices 17592186127175] [:db/retract 17592186127171 :answer-field/choices 17592186127176] [:db/retract 17592186127177 :answer-field/choices 17592186127178] [:db/retract 17592186127177 :answer-field/choices 17592186127179] [:db/retract 17592186127177 :answer-field/choices 17592186127180] [:db/retract 17592186127177 :answer-field/choices 17592186127181] [:db/retract 17592186127177 :answer-field/choices 17592186127182] {:db/id 17592186129193 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-266a83b4-e89e-5341-ae78-39b530317ac4" "question-1f526958-5ff7-5f1e-b97a-1125f849ae32"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129194 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-94516772-c0e6-5471-a227-05c6f1da1b17" "question-d88f3d27-3ac2-554c-ada9-11e1bf695d44"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268857 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/01/019f0f9f83ddf323d543d109677335cb3ae72a200baf834453a0eca16a9353ba.png)\n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d2/d222e86f6af7c3ed2b35711114c9062d6f0c9b0f2551aa44cac33318c6e5813f.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,x+2\\le y\\le \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2\\le y\\le x+2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{x+2}^{{x}^{2}/2+2}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/2+2}^{x+2}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-266a83b4-e89e-5341-ae78-39b530317ac4" :question/id #uuid "266a83b4-e89e-5341-ae78-39b530317ac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-132296" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $y=\\frac{3x}{2}+6,$ $y=-3x-3$ and $y=0.$ Express ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type I regions." :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/8d/8dad5cdaa9fdcaf0293773c53a0b5ab58d7d98aea0099af0a9ba616e72759129.png)\n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0b5bfe05214df5f59a563677bd6b4f7dfd85be4d8e27fd4d7b31f87a679cd2b.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le x\\le -2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{3x}{2}+6\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le -1,\\,0\\le y\\le -3x-3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{\\int }_{0}^{3x/2+6}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{-3x-3}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-467e7482-236e-50bd-9069-2e1ac24cf7f7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-467e7482-236e-50bd-9069-2e1ac24cf7f7" :answer-field/id #uuid "467e7482-236e-50bd-9069-2e1ac24cf7f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0e63baf8-b0a3-52c1-a22b-545b52cc0260" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b67a2061-2ce8-5628-bc8b-fc965822fa6f" "answer-8d5774ae-41bf-5890-9a15-9b50552548d3" "answer-4acb1ecd-66c4-5afd-b0a9-c1e6beea6f6b" "answer-0e63baf8-b0a3-52c1-a22b-545b52cc0260" "answer-c549c79d-1614-52e0-816d-7b47adc5ec4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b67a2061-2ce8-5628-bc8b-fc965822fa6f" :answer/id #uuid "b67a2061-2ce8-5628-bc8b-fc965822fa6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{3x/2+6}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-3x-3}^{0}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d5774ae-41bf-5890-9a15-9b50552548d3" :answer/id #uuid "8d5774ae-41bf-5890-9a15-9b50552548d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{3x/2+6}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{-3x-3}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4acb1ecd-66c4-5afd-b0a9-c1e6beea6f6b" :answer/id #uuid "4acb1ecd-66c4-5afd-b0a9-c1e6beea6f6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{\\int }_{0}^{3x/2+6}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{-3x-3}^{0}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e63baf8-b0a3-52c1-a22b-545b52cc0260" :answer/id #uuid "0e63baf8-b0a3-52c1-a22b-545b52cc0260" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{\\int }_{0}^{3x/2+6}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{-3x-3}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c549c79d-1614-52e0-816d-7b47adc5ec4c" :answer/id #uuid "c549c79d-1614-52e0-816d-7b47adc5ec4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{\\int }_{3x/2+6}^{0}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{-3x-3}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129615 :question/problem "![](images/7e/7e979ac75636efa00cbc32ee92eff1a5fc1da3cea19378fbf5a39a5cc954ff45.png)\n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2}$ and lines $y=7x,$ $y=x+12,$ as shown in the picture. Express the double integral of the function $f(x,y)$ over the region $D$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type I regions." :question/worked-solution "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ee/ee6fd6a0b89a879d5803a98fda7e80a93926566995bea5f47a05aa7ab2986375.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 7x\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+12\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{7x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+12}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186127171 :answer-field/correct "answer-acfce3f4-00a0-50c8-a775-d7fb78414a0e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-28816995-f0a0-5137-b44e-6c90cb7a7506" "answer-eed3c083-742f-5564-b657-509747487ec1" "answer-acfce3f4-00a0-50c8-a775-d7fb78414a0e" "answer-33826145-0171-51a4-acf8-48226c29de7e" "answer-a8c31b71-e03d-55bb-ad32-043d29658c47"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28816995-f0a0-5137-b44e-6c90cb7a7506" :answer/id #uuid "28816995-f0a0-5137-b44e-6c90cb7a7506" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x+12}^{7x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eed3c083-742f-5564-b657-509747487ec1" :answer/id #uuid "eed3c083-742f-5564-b657-509747487ec1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{7x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{7x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acfce3f4-00a0-50c8-a775-d7fb78414a0e" :answer/id #uuid "acfce3f4-00a0-50c8-a775-d7fb78414a0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{7x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+12}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33826145-0171-51a4-acf8-48226c29de7e" :answer/id #uuid "33826145-0171-51a4-acf8-48226c29de7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+12}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{7x}^{{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8c31b71-e03d-55bb-ad32-043d29658c47" :answer/id #uuid "a8c31b71-e03d-55bb-ad32-043d29658c47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+12}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+12}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1f526958-5ff7-5f1e-b97a-1125f849ae32" :question/id #uuid "1f526958-5ff7-5f1e-b97a-1125f849ae32" :question/math-academy-id "q-82075" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the curve $x=3-{y}^{2}$ and the line $y=1-x.$ \nExpress ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type I regions." :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/8a/8a46756cd4842caf7977ef72ae05912a635843a06382b21136362455a94369e4.png)\n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.   Notice that we're using that $x=3-{y}^{2}$ can be split into $y=\\pm \\sqrt{3-x}.$ \n\n![](images/43/43da7a61d7a4bff15274cdacbf1f3265c097edb8740583c69463460467f8100f.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,1-x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{3-x}\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 3,\\,-\\sqrt{3-x}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{3-x}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{1-x}^{\\sqrt{3-x}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3-x}}^{\\sqrt{3-x}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-54e9c707-1747-52ad-abb8-366afb9bc3dd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-54e9c707-1747-52ad-abb8-366afb9bc3dd" :answer-field/id #uuid "54e9c707-1747-52ad-abb8-366afb9bc3dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8b2918b4-5b1a-5e09-96c3-37123de1ba55" :answer-field/choices ["answer-27c7f92a-5c64-5f2f-a12c-3e4b7e56d6f6" "answer-8b2918b4-5b1a-5e09-96c3-37123de1ba55" "answer-8c2300f0-baa5-5212-b5f7-d261c57b46a7" "answer-ddf08033-d820-539a-8135-8cad5b26ce10" "answer-e9e13229-a93d-5417-8009-b20a01b65756"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27c7f92a-5c64-5f2f-a12c-3e4b7e56d6f6" :answer/id #uuid "27c7f92a-5c64-5f2f-a12c-3e4b7e56d6f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{1-x}^{3-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b2918b4-5b1a-5e09-96c3-37123de1ba55" :answer/id #uuid "8b2918b4-5b1a-5e09-96c3-37123de1ba55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{1-x}^{\\sqrt{3-x}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3-x}}^{\\sqrt{3-x}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c2300f0-baa5-5212-b5f7-d261c57b46a7" :answer/id #uuid "8c2300f0-baa5-5212-b5f7-d261c57b46a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{1-x}^{\\sqrt{3-{x}^{2}}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3-x}}^{\\sqrt{3-x}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddf08033-d820-539a-8135-8cad5b26ce10" :answer/id #uuid "ddf08033-d820-539a-8135-8cad5b26ce10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{1-x}^{\\sqrt{3-{x}^{2}}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3-x}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9e13229-a93d-5417-8009-b20a01b65756" :answer/id #uuid "e9e13229-a93d-5417-8009-b20a01b65756" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{1-x}^{\\sqrt{3-x}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{x-3}^{3-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186388258 :question/worked-solution "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/0e/0e69b1cb480b87216273db4336adff57cbf9d9bc450aac4b0cdb58b1ae63f6f7.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-3\\le x\\le 0,\\,-\\frac{2x}{3}\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{3}+3\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{4x}{3}\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{3}+3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}{\\int }_{-2x/3}^{x/3+3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{4x/3}^{x/3+3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268861 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our equations in terms of $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-x & \\,⟹\\,x=-y \\\\ y=6-4x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{y}{4} \\\\ y=2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/08/08237d1ca2e01d0cf620dfa06a4265d8053b7ee34d0b65036aad7d5734726e1c.png)\n\n \n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/c7/c756dc7924fc4c116601da907eb3b767b8a40647e063c146dad83b0ea660933b.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 0,\\,-y\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{y}{4}\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{y}{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-y}^{3/2-y/4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{3/2-y/4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186394366 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our equations in terms of $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\sqrt{x-2}+1 & \\,⟹\\,x=(y-1)^{2}+2 \\\\ y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x & \\,⟹\\,x=4-2y \\\\ y=8-2x & \\,⟹\\,x=4-\\frac{1}{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/3f/3f8e3ccfeac236d7fe9a502f599c61c404607a55ae809ec142019a3f881e1568.png)\n\n  \n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/31/3148cb0f499564db9cbfd6b635fef81dc47298789241af4a243c32dbf4dc8247.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,4-2y\\le x\\le 4-\\frac{1}{2}y\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,(y-1)^{2}+2\\le x\\le 4-\\frac{1}{2}y\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-2y}^{4-y/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{(y-1)^{2}+2}^{4-y/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-94516772-c0e6-5471-a227-05c6f1da1b17" :question/id #uuid "94516772-c0e6-5471-a227-05c6f1da1b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-132380" :question/problem "![](images/48/4826bc628c1bd8f4293fd5fbf90bf9155658c15336c2c9e86a5e7678dfcf1ca3.png)\n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $y=-x,$ $y=x+4,$ $y=0$ and $y=4,$ as shown in the picture. Express the double integral of the function $f(x,y)$ over the region $D$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions." :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our equations in terms of $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-x & \\,⟹\\,x=-y \\\\ y=x+4 & \\,⟹\\,x=y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/17/17d3ceabf3cd7a052398d8d12b7963c5ca64356605554e3ff43c21c7321e45de.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y-4\\le x\\le -y\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le y\\le 4,\\,-y\\le x\\le y-4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-4}^{-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{-y}^{y-4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7086a944-de0d-51cd-aea2-90dc23497170"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7086a944-de0d-51cd-aea2-90dc23497170" :answer-field/id #uuid "7086a944-de0d-51cd-aea2-90dc23497170" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-75dc7848-feaf-5ac4-b992-3cb45e8de29d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e47d7134-16ad-5275-ae8c-f2d10be54f29" "answer-bbd2d108-8ee6-5f0f-bf18-681b33bcd677" "answer-738c1fa7-1d38-589b-9f4d-22a2183e34e3" "answer-75dc7848-feaf-5ac4-b992-3cb45e8de29d" "answer-6e37823a-7c19-5cf4-956c-1f63183d522e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e47d7134-16ad-5275-ae8c-f2d10be54f29" :answer/id #uuid "e47d7134-16ad-5275-ae8c-f2d10be54f29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y-4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{-y}^{y-4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbd2d108-8ee6-5f0f-bf18-681b33bcd677" :answer/id #uuid "bbd2d108-8ee6-5f0f-bf18-681b33bcd677" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y+4}^{-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{-y}^{y+4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-738c1fa7-1d38-589b-9f4d-22a2183e34e3" :answer/id #uuid "738c1fa7-1d38-589b-9f4d-22a2183e34e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y+4}^{-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{y+4}^{-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75dc7848-feaf-5ac4-b992-3cb45e8de29d" :answer/id #uuid "75dc7848-feaf-5ac4-b992-3cb45e8de29d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-4}^{-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{-y}^{y-4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e37823a-7c19-5cf4-956c-1f63183d522e" :answer/id #uuid "6e37823a-7c19-5cf4-956c-1f63183d522e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-4}^{-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{y-4}^{-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d88f3d27-3ac2-554c-ada9-11e1bf695d44" :question/id #uuid "d88f3d27-3ac2-554c-ada9-11e1bf695d44" :question/math-academy-id "q-82116" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7fa5e4cd0c573ab5841e3ded221486ee2cabf6e00e1493c3de06fe4d9a87e3a7.png)\n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2},$ $y=2x,$ and $y=\\frac{(2x+2)}{3},$ as shown in the picture. Express the double integral of the function $f(x,y)$ over the region $D$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions." :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our equations in terms of $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{2y} \\\\ y=2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ y=\\frac{2x+2}{3} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{3y-2}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/63/63848a26cff0641ac83c73e809028852697212ce64a8cb954815d514922721a1.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{2y}\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{3y-2}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{2y}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{2y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{(3y-2)/2}^{\\sqrt{2y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d14fe408-e710-5a6c-9dc2-c17893127e06"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d14fe408-e710-5a6c-9dc2-c17893127e06" :answer-field/id #uuid "d14fe408-e710-5a6c-9dc2-c17893127e06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-db123738-960e-56c7-b973-f03c45f9e841" :answer-field/choices ["answer-39f60118-786e-5b4f-b742-fa260616a9fa" "answer-db123738-960e-56c7-b973-f03c45f9e841" "answer-e161cf4c-90cd-5345-a9b9-1dfc2d9a1476" "answer-ee55bf76-b835-59b2-b361-2dd5c58bebd6" "answer-55af3ac3-2ce5-50b7-9a85-a4cefb08d592"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39f60118-786e-5b4f-b742-fa260616a9fa" :answer/id #uuid "39f60118-786e-5b4f-b742-fa260616a9fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{2y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{(3y+2)/2}^{\\sqrt{2y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db123738-960e-56c7-b973-f03c45f9e841" :answer/id #uuid "db123738-960e-56c7-b973-f03c45f9e841" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{2y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{(3y-2)/2}^{\\sqrt{2y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e161cf4c-90cd-5345-a9b9-1dfc2d9a1476" :answer/id #uuid "e161cf4c-90cd-5345-a9b9-1dfc2d9a1476" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{\\sqrt{2y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{(3y-2)/2}^{\\sqrt{2y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee55bf76-b835-59b2-b361-2dd5c58bebd6" :answer/id #uuid "ee55bf76-b835-59b2-b361-2dd5c58bebd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/2}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{(2y+2)/3}^{\\sqrt{2y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55af3ac3-2ce5-50b7-9a85-a4cefb08d592" :answer/id #uuid "55af3ac3-2ce5-50b7-9a85-a4cefb08d592" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{(3y+2)/2}^{\\sqrt{2y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351394 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our equations in terms of $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={e}^{x} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,x={e}^{y} \\\\ y=1+e-x & \\,⟹\\,x=1+e-y \\\\ y=1-x & \\,⟹\\,x=1-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/4d/4df7cf2f33520a80270341d4a40b4a83d9f3c11ed8adb69fe1131a42bcd61d5a.png)\n\n    \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,1-y\\le x\\le {e}^{y}\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le e,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\le x\\le 1+e-y\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-y}^{{e}^{y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}^{1+e-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268864 :question/worked-solution "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/33/33a38da74b282f0ec1fb2fae9cb155feb2a5fd39128c7607b80c6b84fedd29b4.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,x-1\\le y\\le 3-x\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,3-x\\le y\\le x-1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2y\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}2y\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}2y\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{x-1}^{3-x}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{3-x}^{x-1}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{x-1}^{3-x}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{3-x}^{x-1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[(3-x)^{2}-(x-1)^{2}]\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}[(x-1)^{2}-(3-x)^{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(8-4x)\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}(4x-8)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(8x-2{x}^{2}){|}_{1}^{2}+(2{x}^{2}-8x){|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =8(2-1)-2({2}^{2}-{1}^{2})+2({4}^{2}-{2}^{2})-8(4-2) \\\\  & =8-6+24-16 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351419 :question/worked-solution "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/64/648121ff2a676f6a472a6e221dffe813eca55f914aed0ca9a285bb0d4cd81ab3.png)\n\n   \n\nAs a result, we get \n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4xy\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}4xy\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}4xy\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}4xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[2x{y}^{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{3}{[2x{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x(9-1)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{3}2x(9-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}16x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{3}18x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{2}+9{x}^{2}{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =8(4-0)+9(9-4) \\\\  & =77.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351418 :question/worked-solution "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/2f/2fcb30778db2f051a7a819ae701808af59ac18d27c25a9c57ce678caa6c3ee3b.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le -x\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2x\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}2x\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}2x\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{-x}2x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x}2x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}2xy{|}_{{x}^{2}}^{-x}\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}2xy{|}_{{x}^{2}}^{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}2x[-x-{x}^{2}]\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x[x-{x}^{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}]}_{-1}^{0}+{[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =((0-(-\\frac{2}{3}(-1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{4}))+((\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{4})-0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129616 :question/problem "${∬}_{|x|+|y|\\le 1}\\,\\,{e}^{x+y}\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/4f/4f4e38950d36bf77ce4b2a907f07b3f9c4b40837729ef644f37ecfbd921aec8a.png)\n\n  \n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b8075ab5905ada88b2a198f73738b2c0c507371cfea32b560c02be4f0185c92f.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-x-1\\le y\\le x+1\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le -x+1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{|x|+|y|\\le 1}{e}^{x+y}\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}{e}^{x+y}\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}{e}^{x+y}\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-x-1}^{x+1}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-x+1}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[{e}^{x+y}]_{-x-1}^{x+1}\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{e}^{x+y}]_{x-1}^{-x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({e}^{2x+1}-{e}^{-1})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(e-{e}^{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2x+1}}{2}-{e}^{-1}x]}_{-1}^{0}+{[ex-\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{e}{2}-(\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{2}+{e}^{-1})]+[(e-\\frac{e}{2})+\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{2}] \\\\  & =e-{e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186127177 :answer-field/correct "answer-830d9a09-4b3d-5488-ab8b-7407c02fb4fe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7da24e9f-953b-5b8d-9fb3-433072fccab1" "answer-d10e18e2-81c4-5858-b24a-9e24eac04a78" "answer-bf4fcd37-0cd2-54cf-8269-3de8bbb9ae1f" "answer-5426c146-fb3d-500e-b01e-5ede41135cc4" "answer-830d9a09-4b3d-5488-ab8b-7407c02fb4fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7da24e9f-953b-5b8d-9fb3-433072fccab1" :answer/id #uuid "7da24e9f-953b-5b8d-9fb3-433072fccab1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(e-{e}^{-1})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d10e18e2-81c4-5858-b24a-9e24eac04a78" :answer/id #uuid "d10e18e2-81c4-5858-b24a-9e24eac04a78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{e-{e}^{-1}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf4fcd37-0cd2-54cf-8269-3de8bbb9ae1f" :answer/id #uuid "bf4fcd37-0cd2-54cf-8269-3de8bbb9ae1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}-{e}^{-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5426c146-fb3d-500e-b01e-5ede41135cc4" :answer/id #uuid "5426c146-fb3d-500e-b01e-5ede41135cc4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e-\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-830d9a09-4b3d-5488-ab8b-7407c02fb4fe" :answer/id #uuid "830d9a09-4b3d-5488-ab8b-7407c02fb4fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e-{e}^{-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186323935 :question/worked-solution "The region $D$ can be partitioned into three type II regions ${D}_{1},$ ${D}_{2},$ and ${D}_{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/b5/b58fc3909d5be467999053336f67d41b88b43c984208c60f3d46ee8b7479df58.png)\n\nAs a result, we get \n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,-2\\le x\\le -1\\},\n$$\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le x\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{3}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-2\\le x\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}{x}^{2}y\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}{x}^{2}y\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{3}}{x}^{2}y\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{y\\text{ }\\,{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{-2}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{y\\text{ }\\,{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\frac{y\\text{ }\\,{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{-2}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{7y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{7y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{16y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{64}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{71}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130400 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to evaluate the double integral  \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} 2(x+y)\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the finite region bounded by the lines $x=0, \\, y=1, \\, y=2, \\, y=3-x,$ and $y=x.$ How do we proceed?\n\nTo evaluate an integral like this, we carry out the following steps: \n\n**Step 1.** Sketch the region $D$. This allows us to determine the precise set-up of our integral. \n\nIn this example, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/0a/0a90a4f377327f5293308f1e08ac6596f1c27b9bd47a614d6d7cb1c5dbfec33c.png)\n\n**Step 2.** Split (if necessary) the region $D$ into several type I or II regions.\n\nFrom the diagram above, we see that our region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions $D_1$ and $D_2,$ as follows.\n\n![](images/a9/a93e1a0a0a7f9c104cb5d380b2a7ff98d6712ef0d2d67b74130da5dcf1701443.png)\n\n \n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x  \\leq 1, \\quad 1 \\leq y \\leq 2\\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD_2= \\{(x,y)\\: : \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3/2,  \\quad  x \\leq y \\leq 3-x\\}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Set up and evaluate the integral using the additivity property of double integrals. We recall the following fact:\n\n*If $D$ is the union of two sub-regions, $D_1, D_2$, that don't overlap then,*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{D_1}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A + \\iint\\limits_{D_2}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nSo, finally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}2(x+y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx+{\\int }_{1}^{3/2}{\\int }_{x}^{3-x}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129341 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054710 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cbfb11c2-4e8c-533c-b361-e0d38d677419" "question-c458355f-4593-5f0b-9073-264d9e39547a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054711 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e00081d5-c6bc-5ebe-9c4d-19fd46daf244" "question-17858799-d724-5cb1-b5ca-0839a5bde618"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054349 :question/worked-solution "The polynomial $1+2x^2 - 8x$ has three terms, so it is a trinomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $2,$ so it is quadratic. \n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a quadratic trinomial." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054712 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2c70515f-461b-5406-a00b-651a86f2659d" "question-298a3ab5-9f3b-5907-82bb-2239de27222c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054350 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The sum of $x^2+2$ and $3x$ is a trinomial.\n\nII. The sum of $5x^2+4x$ and $-4x$ is a trinomial.\n\nIII. The sum of a monomial and a binomial is **always** a trinomial." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n(x^2+2) + (3x) = x^2+3x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a trinomial.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n(5x^2+4x) + (-4x) = 5x^2,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a trinomial (it's a monomial).\n\n- Statement III is false. The sum of a monomial and a binomial *could be* a trinomial (see, for example, the statement I).\n\nHowever, the sum of a monomial and a binomial is *not always* a trinomial (see, for example, the statement II).\n\nSo, the sum of a monomial and a binomial is *not always* a trinomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-cbfb11c2-4e8c-533c-b361-e0d38d677419" :question/id #uuid "cbfb11c2-4e8c-533c-b361-e0d38d677419" :question/math-academy-id "q-7093" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is a binomial?\n\nI. ${x}^{2}+x+4$\n\nII. ${x}^{3}+2x$\n\nIII. $2{x}^{2}+5$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each expression.\n\n- Expression I is not a binomial. The polynomial ${x}^{2}+x+4$ has three terms, so it is a trinomial, not a binomial.\n\n- Expression II is a binomial. The polynomial ${x}^{3}+2x$ has two terms, so it is indeed a binomial.\n\n- Expression III is a binomial. The polynomial $2{x}^{2}+5$ has two terms, so it is indeed a binomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c1f21980-3e87-514c-b8e8-57d381f8f745"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c1f21980-3e87-514c-b8e8-57d381f8f745" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1f21980-3e87-514c-b8e8-57d381f8f745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-deeca579-87f4-5041-8d85-98b282b1b739" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4030a895-1e66-5567-9cf1-a089ba280517" "answer-b4fd5d66-54ca-588d-b806-09f01fa4742e" "answer-deeca579-87f4-5041-8d85-98b282b1b739" "answer-af3a239c-f40a-5366-a917-bd1f3e7efdfa" "answer-a515a49b-c1c4-5692-8347-b6479c4ec534"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4030a895-1e66-5567-9cf1-a089ba280517" :answer/id #uuid "4030a895-1e66-5567-9cf1-a089ba280517" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4fd5d66-54ca-588d-b806-09f01fa4742e" :answer/id #uuid "b4fd5d66-54ca-588d-b806-09f01fa4742e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-deeca579-87f4-5041-8d85-98b282b1b739" :answer/id #uuid "deeca579-87f4-5041-8d85-98b282b1b739" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af3a239c-f40a-5366-a917-bd1f3e7efdfa" :answer/id #uuid "af3a239c-f40a-5366-a917-bd1f3e7efdfa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a515a49b-c1c4-5692-8347-b6479c4ec534" :answer/id #uuid "a515a49b-c1c4-5692-8347-b6479c4ec534" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c458355f-4593-5f0b-9073-264d9e39547a" :question/id #uuid "c458355f-4593-5f0b-9073-264d9e39547a" :question/math-academy-id "q-44689" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is a trinomial?" :question/worked-solution "The polynomial $8{v}^{4}-10v+19$ has three terms, so it is a trinomial." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c868bc62-8564-5c18-b969-026f902a99df"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c868bc62-8564-5c18-b969-026f902a99df" :answer-field/id #uuid "c868bc62-8564-5c18-b969-026f902a99df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d1fc08fe-34c3-5d2d-9202-e53ffd076a41" :answer-field/choices ["answer-69c33b59-e7fe-5509-b0c5-fc05809c820b" "answer-733502e9-7cbf-5c73-af78-8fe171b45206" "answer-5f24f2ec-e033-5b76-a8c2-c2967b3787e0" "answer-d1fc08fe-34c3-5d2d-9202-e53ffd076a41" "answer-64421459-2b4c-56e6-9391-8985a7eef3ea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69c33b59-e7fe-5509-b0c5-fc05809c820b" :answer/id #uuid "69c33b59-e7fe-5509-b0c5-fc05809c820b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{z}^{4}-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-733502e9-7cbf-5c73-af78-8fe171b45206" :answer/id #uuid "733502e9-7cbf-5c73-af78-8fe171b45206" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6{v}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f24f2ec-e033-5b76-a8c2-c2967b3787e0" :answer/id #uuid "5f24f2ec-e033-5b76-a8c2-c2967b3787e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{u}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1fc08fe-34c3-5d2d-9202-e53ffd076a41" :answer/id #uuid "d1fc08fe-34c3-5d2d-9202-e53ffd076a41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8{v}^{4}-10v+19" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64421459-2b4c-56e6-9391-8985a7eef3ea" :answer/id #uuid "64421459-2b4c-56e6-9391-8985a7eef3ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{p}^{2}-12p" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e00081d5-c6bc-5ebe-9c4d-19fd46daf244" :question/id #uuid "e00081d5-c6bc-5ebe-9c4d-19fd46daf244" :question/math-academy-id "q-111225" :question/problem "Categorize the polynomial $3{h}^{4}$ based on its degree and the number of terms it has." :question/worked-solution "The polynomial $3{h}^{4}$ has only one term, so it is a monomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $4,$ so it is a quartic.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a quartic monomial." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5117de3a-07aa-5519-87b1-38042bdf315a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5117de3a-07aa-5519-87b1-38042bdf315a" :answer-field/id #uuid "5117de3a-07aa-5519-87b1-38042bdf315a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b9e3a7dd-30c5-5a0e-ad80-71e2058da5ee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-edff33b5-ad53-5c02-89fa-091a3ea8308e" "answer-7aa48fab-715e-5ede-99d8-5d064c0e6739" "answer-b15c50f0-be03-5370-b810-2fe0777248de" "answer-b9e3a7dd-30c5-5a0e-ad80-71e2058da5ee" "answer-7e58282b-3e4e-5722-ac04-ace8c4d48e16"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edff33b5-ad53-5c02-89fa-091a3ea8308e" :answer/id #uuid "edff33b5-ad53-5c02-89fa-091a3ea8308e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "cubic binomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7aa48fab-715e-5ede-99d8-5d064c0e6739" :answer/id #uuid "7aa48fab-715e-5ede-99d8-5d064c0e6739" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "cubic monomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b15c50f0-be03-5370-b810-2fe0777248de" :answer/id #uuid "b15c50f0-be03-5370-b810-2fe0777248de" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "quartic binomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9e3a7dd-30c5-5a0e-ad80-71e2058da5ee" :answer/id #uuid "b9e3a7dd-30c5-5a0e-ad80-71e2058da5ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "quartic monomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e58282b-3e4e-5722-ac04-ace8c4d48e16" :answer/id #uuid "7e58282b-3e4e-5722-ac04-ace8c4d48e16" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "quartic trinomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-17858799-d724-5cb1-b5ca-0839a5bde618" :question/id #uuid "17858799-d724-5cb1-b5ca-0839a5bde618" :question/math-academy-id "q-51577" :question/problem "Classify the polynomial $7{z}^{3}$ according to its degree and the number of terms." :question/worked-solution "The polynomial $7{z}^{3}$ has only one term, so it is a monomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $3,$ so it is a cubic.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a cubic monomial." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d25ac4d7-d0a8-5f24-a449-c058b7541cbd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d25ac4d7-d0a8-5f24-a449-c058b7541cbd" :answer-field/id #uuid "d25ac4d7-d0a8-5f24-a449-c058b7541cbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-84eb7d9d-437b-5a61-97af-ae36dcddbd70" :answer-field/choices ["answer-84eb7d9d-437b-5a61-97af-ae36dcddbd70" "answer-84e34c6f-8516-506e-b164-3ae3ae7a88b9" "answer-a6e687a8-8c62-5b90-a0aa-098d46aeb6ea" "answer-7dc02b63-7f40-5d69-bcd0-834b2521e040" "answer-561dd74e-9ffd-5661-8f58-687b9aaf2221"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84eb7d9d-437b-5a61-97af-ae36dcddbd70" :answer/id #uuid "84eb7d9d-437b-5a61-97af-ae36dcddbd70" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Cubic monomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84e34c6f-8516-506e-b164-3ae3ae7a88b9" :answer/id #uuid "84e34c6f-8516-506e-b164-3ae3ae7a88b9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Cubic trinomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6e687a8-8c62-5b90-a0aa-098d46aeb6ea" :answer/id #uuid "a6e687a8-8c62-5b90-a0aa-098d46aeb6ea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Quadratic monomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7dc02b63-7f40-5d69-bcd0-834b2521e040" :answer/id #uuid "7dc02b63-7f40-5d69-bcd0-834b2521e040" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Quartic monomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-561dd74e-9ffd-5661-8f58-687b9aaf2221" :answer/id #uuid "561dd74e-9ffd-5661-8f58-687b9aaf2221" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Quartic trinomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2c70515f-461b-5406-a00b-651a86f2659d" :question/id #uuid "2c70515f-461b-5406-a00b-651a86f2659d" :question/math-academy-id "q-152252" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $5{x}^{2}-7x+1$ is cubic trinomial.\n\nII. ${x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x$ is cubic trinomial.\n\nIII. **Some** trinomials are cubic." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since the degree of the trinomial $5{x}^{2}-7x+1$ is $2,$ it is a quadratic trinomial.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the degree of the trinomial ${x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x$ is $3,$ it is a cubic trinomial.\n\n- Statement III is true. A trinomial *could be* cubic (see, for example, statement II). \n\n However, a trinomial is *not always* cubic (see, for example, statement I). \n\n So, some trinomials are cubic.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-68adcac4-f714-5f55-81da-9c003d947562"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-68adcac4-f714-5f55-81da-9c003d947562" :answer-field/id #uuid "68adcac4-f714-5f55-81da-9c003d947562" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8efd74f5-cafb-5856-a477-190813a8a4ff" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c9db9c56-7c9d-5fbd-9ab7-859523a2dbc1" "answer-7ef2bd94-3507-5818-a8e2-21b4e3d5daaa" "answer-031cfe8a-aedb-5c47-85b8-b78d424c9253" "answer-8efd74f5-cafb-5856-a477-190813a8a4ff" "answer-4cb13528-7dc2-5e8c-81d3-76237471c1a9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9db9c56-7c9d-5fbd-9ab7-859523a2dbc1" :answer/id #uuid "c9db9c56-7c9d-5fbd-9ab7-859523a2dbc1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ef2bd94-3507-5818-a8e2-21b4e3d5daaa" :answer/id #uuid "7ef2bd94-3507-5818-a8e2-21b4e3d5daaa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-031cfe8a-aedb-5c47-85b8-b78d424c9253" :answer/id #uuid "031cfe8a-aedb-5c47-85b8-b78d424c9253" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8efd74f5-cafb-5856-a477-190813a8a4ff" :answer/id #uuid "8efd74f5-cafb-5856-a477-190813a8a4ff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cb13528-7dc2-5e8c-81d3-76237471c1a9" :answer/id #uuid "4cb13528-7dc2-5e8c-81d3-76237471c1a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-298a3ab5-9f3b-5907-82bb-2239de27222c" :question/id #uuid "298a3ab5-9f3b-5907-82bb-2239de27222c" :question/math-academy-id "q-152438" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The product of $7x$ and $3{x}^{2}$ is a binomial.\n\nII. The product of $4x$ and $5$ is a monomial.\n\nIII. The product of two monomials is **always** a binomial." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n(7x)\\times (3{x}^{2})=21{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a binomial (it's a monomial).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n(4x)\\times (5)=20x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a monomial.\n\n- Statement III is false. There are products of two monomials that are not binomials (see, for example, statement I and II).\n\nIn fact, the product of two monomials is *never* a binomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-62c88c7f-5c96-5f04-a40f-dca9bee2c854"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-62c88c7f-5c96-5f04-a40f-dca9bee2c854" :answer-field/id #uuid "62c88c7f-5c96-5f04-a40f-dca9bee2c854" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5cb50a14-8d14-5abf-bbba-8ffff9b40b1f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0ae009f5-4ad8-554c-9afe-f7df0d60df6c" "answer-ec85cf8b-2ad9-57c7-abee-ddf2ba3c9e29" "answer-d6d1b1b3-57ce-5b6f-aeae-962b95ce3397" "answer-5cb50a14-8d14-5abf-bbba-8ffff9b40b1f" "answer-e11a5ec7-6258-52b7-931d-7cf5782a0e9c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ae009f5-4ad8-554c-9afe-f7df0d60df6c" :answer/id #uuid "0ae009f5-4ad8-554c-9afe-f7df0d60df6c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec85cf8b-2ad9-57c7-abee-ddf2ba3c9e29" :answer/id #uuid "ec85cf8b-2ad9-57c7-abee-ddf2ba3c9e29" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6d1b1b3-57ce-5b6f-aeae-962b95ce3397" :answer/id #uuid "d6d1b1b3-57ce-5b6f-aeae-962b95ce3397" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5cb50a14-8d14-5abf-bbba-8ffff9b40b1f" :answer/id #uuid "5cb50a14-8d14-5abf-bbba-8ffff9b40b1f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e11a5ec7-6258-52b7-931d-7cf5782a0e9c" :answer/id #uuid "e11a5ec7-6258-52b7-931d-7cf5782a0e9c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055997 :tutorial/content "Polynomials with one, two, or three terms have special names.\n\n- A polynomial with one term is called a **monomial**. For example:\n \n\n$$\n1, \\qquad y,\t\\qquad\t2x^3,\t\\qquad\t5a^{4}\n$$\n\nHowever, the following expressions are not monomials because they can't be called polynomials:\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{y},\t\\qquad\t5t^{-1},\t\\qquad \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad \t2z^{1/4}\n$$\n\n- A polynomial with two terms is called a **binomial**. For example:\n\n$$\nx+3,\t\\qquad\t7n^2 +n,\t\\qquad\tp^{32} -1\n$$\n\n- A polynomial with three terms is called a **trinomial**. For example:\n \n\n$$\nz^2 -z +1,\t\\qquad\tx^3 -2x -1,\t\\qquad\t2c^{4} - 3c^{3} +4c^2\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055998 :tutorial/content "Each type of polynomial of degree between $0$ and $5$ has a specific name. These are shown in the table below.\n\n| Degree | Name | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | Constant | $7$ |\n| $1$ | Linear | $3x + 2$ |\n| $2$ | Quadratic | $2x^2 - 6x + 1$ |\n| $3$ | Cubic | $x^3 + 4x^2 + 9x$ |\n| $4$ | Quartic | $3x^4 - 5x^2$ |\n| $5$ | Quintic | $7x^5 + 3x^4 - x^4 + x - 6$ |\n\nWe can combine these with the names we learned earlier. \n\nFor example:\n\n- $2x+1$ is a *linear binomial* because its degree equals $1,$ and it has $2$ terms.\n\n- $x^2$ is a *quadratic monomial* because its degree equals $2,$ and it has $1$ term.\n\n- $2x^3+x+1$ is a *cubic trinomial* because its degree equals $3,$ and it has $3$ terms.\n\nIf a polynomial contains more than three terms, then we refer to it by its degree only.\n\nFor example:\n\n- $x^3+2x^2+x-1$ is a *cubic polynomial.*\n\n- $x^4+x^3+x^2+x+1$ is a *quartic polynomial.*" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054924 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198390 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fd38a4fc-c1e2-5044-998c-eee65f3245ff"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-fd38a4fc-c1e2-5044-998c-eee65f3245ff" :question/id #uuid "fd38a4fc-c1e2-5044-998c-eee65f3245ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-162559" :question/problem "![](images/80/804976bf326ed561ff8e7b4f5a6c65361039a686a4449edfdf8e6f497ee5cbda.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,xy-z,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,xy-z,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the right and left sides. \n\n![](images/de/de9eb8381df05d2d5e6222a51b0ccbc5729e522df94a4aace570881d1226a1ca.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 2.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,xy-z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,x-z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =x-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}x-z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x-z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x-z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-xz]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}-z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}z-\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 2.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,xy-z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[x]_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =(-1)+2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2652e0ca-544c-51d5-827f-9347b3d96ac0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2652e0ca-544c-51d5-827f-9347b3d96ac0" :answer-field/id #uuid "2652e0ca-544c-51d5-827f-9347b3d96ac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0de413f9-cd7a-535a-8ffa-46d5c7da302d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2a020e56-2589-5e05-8a71-55eaf678b2b0" "answer-5d0e7c8f-f911-5f64-b0c6-19e7a9add138" "answer-0de413f9-cd7a-535a-8ffa-46d5c7da302d" "answer-7c27cb5a-7606-5667-9a19-07de7ad9160b" "answer-720df5c2-6cd7-54e9-a9af-ea4b64405c63"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a020e56-2589-5e05-8a71-55eaf678b2b0" :answer/id #uuid "2a020e56-2589-5e05-8a71-55eaf678b2b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d0e7c8f-f911-5f64-b0c6-19e7a9add138" :answer/id #uuid "5d0e7c8f-f911-5f64-b0c6-19e7a9add138" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0de413f9-cd7a-535a-8ffa-46d5c7da302d" :answer/id #uuid "0de413f9-cd7a-535a-8ffa-46d5c7da302d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c27cb5a-7606-5667-9a19-07de7ad9160b" :answer/id #uuid "7c27cb5a-7606-5667-9a19-07de7ad9160b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-720df5c2-6cd7-54e9-a9af-ea4b64405c63" :answer/id #uuid "720df5c2-6cd7-54e9-a9af-ea4b64405c63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-41a71fe0-482d-55e4-85d0-de94d279d70a" :activity/id #uuid "41a71fe0-482d-55e4-85d0-de94d279d70a" :activity/title "Flux Through Closed Surfaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049315 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-dbec28a3-ebf2-534e-a733-aa0d3d96980d" "step-58a9eb2b-d540-5d6b-9d55-2b38cf818ec8" "step-edcfd322-cba5-5279-917b-33694a5197bb" "step-17afeff9-39f1-5994-86da-13a9c620fd75" "step-01b6cab6-7e9c-559f-bbcf-44a96f585fe0"] :activity/first-step "step-dbec28a3-ebf2-534e-a733-aa0d3d96980d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-dbec28a3-ebf2-534e-a733-aa0d3d96980d" :step/id #uuid "dbec28a3-ebf2-534e-a733-aa0d3d96980d" :step/content 17592186198601 :step/next "step-58a9eb2b-d540-5d6b-9d55-2b38cf818ec8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-58a9eb2b-d540-5d6b-9d55-2b38cf818ec8" :step/id #uuid "58a9eb2b-d540-5d6b-9d55-2b38cf818ec8" :step/content 17592186278424 :step/next "step-edcfd322-cba5-5279-917b-33694a5197bb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-edcfd322-cba5-5279-917b-33694a5197bb" :step/id #uuid "edcfd322-cba5-5279-917b-33694a5197bb" :step/content 17592186278431 :step/next "step-17afeff9-39f1-5994-86da-13a9c620fd75" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-17afeff9-39f1-5994-86da-13a9c620fd75" :step/id #uuid "17afeff9-39f1-5994-86da-13a9c620fd75" :step/content 17592186278432 :step/next "step-01b6cab6-7e9c-559f-bbcf-44a96f585fe0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-01b6cab6-7e9c-559f-bbcf-44a96f585fe0" :step/id #uuid "01b6cab6-7e9c-559f-bbcf-44a96f585fe0" :step/content "question-fd38a4fc-c1e2-5044-998c-eee65f3245ff" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186326747 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8+4\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-8+4\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}+(8+4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-2+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=4\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(-2+\\text{i})(-2-\\text{i})}{(1)(1)}=5.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351202 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-2}{2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-2(2-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})}{6}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351255 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(-4)(5) & =0 \\\\ 21-7\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(41)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(5)(5)}{(-2+4\\text{i})(-2-4\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186272074 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5-4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+4\\text{i} & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-2+4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(2+4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5-4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1+2\\text{i}}{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186272077 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(4)(-5) & =0 \\\\ 21-7\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(41)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -5 & -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-5)(-5)}{(2+4\\text{i})(2-4\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-9c7a70fd-abbe-516b-95be-41d0d36b0d99" :activity/id #uuid "9c7a70fd-abbe-516b-95be-41d0d36b0d99" :activity/title "Finding Complex Eigenvectors of Real 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048973 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-4638cfef-2e48-5d74-ab48-bf41cd68013c" "step-532bd258-dd75-52e0-854c-9fc4d84a23df" "step-752a7cb9-f738-503d-976c-4799c9cb6901" "step-94fc7cb2-a156-54c9-af1e-6b820bdce7bf" "step-f2a7d45a-7b9b-5db0-81a3-fa5d6f0775a5"] :activity/first-step "step-4638cfef-2e48-5d74-ab48-bf41cd68013c" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-4638cfef-2e48-5d74-ab48-bf41cd68013c" :step/id #uuid "4638cfef-2e48-5d74-ab48-bf41cd68013c" :step/content 17592186326747 :step/next "step-532bd258-dd75-52e0-854c-9fc4d84a23df" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-532bd258-dd75-52e0-854c-9fc4d84a23df" :step/id #uuid "532bd258-dd75-52e0-854c-9fc4d84a23df" :step/content 17592186351202 :step/next "step-752a7cb9-f738-503d-976c-4799c9cb6901" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-752a7cb9-f738-503d-976c-4799c9cb6901" :step/id #uuid "752a7cb9-f738-503d-976c-4799c9cb6901" :step/content 17592186351255 :step/next "step-94fc7cb2-a156-54c9-af1e-6b820bdce7bf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-94fc7cb2-a156-54c9-af1e-6b820bdce7bf" :step/id #uuid "94fc7cb2-a156-54c9-af1e-6b820bdce7bf" :step/content 17592186272074 :step/next "step-f2a7d45a-7b9b-5db0-81a3-fa5d6f0775a5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f2a7d45a-7b9b-5db0-81a3-fa5d6f0775a5" :step/id #uuid "f2a7d45a-7b9b-5db0-81a3-fa5d6f0775a5" :step/content 17592186272077 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133738 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fde23c1a-e66d-5e7a-9ea9-da78d578c05e" "question-27c95843-92ef-5235-8a90-11a7d406c25c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133511 :question/problem "What is the value of $70^3?$" :question/worked-solution "To work out $70^3,$ we multiply $70$ by itself and then multiply the result by $70$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{70}^{3} & =70\\cdot 70\\cdot 70 \\\\  & =4\\,900\\cdot 70 &  \\\\  & =343\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133739 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e975f59b-8241-5893-a620-db23fb8091f7" "question-4b7f00c3-bb1b-59ab-b7ad-729a50e10070" "question-b4c4f144-c46d-561f-b5f5-844ef14dbc89" "question-11153da4-48d0-5e46-974b-c9f9e9d4e7ed"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133512 :question/problem "Calculate the value of $\\left(-13\\right)^3.$" :question/worked-solution "To work out $\\left(-13\\right)^3,$ we multiply $-13$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-13$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-13)^{3} & =\\overset{169}{\\overset{⏞}{(-13)\\cdot (-13)}}\\cdot (-13) \\\\  & =169\\cdot (-13) \\\\  & =-2\\,197\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133740 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-da278c6f-6480-5739-b071-7c465347a792" "question-bcb80b5f-d39a-5f5e-9109-13ded54ba26d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133513 :question/worked-solution "To work out $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3,$ we multiply $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ by itself and then multiply the result by $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 3\\cdot 3}{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133741 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cd92d947-d1af-50e2-a5d9-2edba3de1406" "question-b50cc60c-3830-586d-ac40-7d3e6216a024"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133514 :question/problem "What is the cube of $(-0.5)?$" :question/worked-solution "To work out $\\left(-0.5\\right)^3,$ we multiply $-0.5$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-0.5$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-0.5)^{3} & =\\overset{0.25}{\\overset{⏞}{(-0.5)\\cdot (-0.5)}}\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & =0.25\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & =-0.125\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-fde23c1a-e66d-5e7a-9ea9-da78d578c05e" :question/id #uuid "fde23c1a-e66d-5e7a-9ea9-da78d578c05e" :question/math-academy-id "q-43228" :question/problem "${15}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "To work out ${15}^{3},$ we multiply $15$ by itself and then multiply the result by $15$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{15}^{3} & =15\\cdot 15\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =225\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =3\\,375\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-72cb9bf0-ccb6-559e-9563-0a1c32c36bf0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-72cb9bf0-ccb6-559e-9563-0a1c32c36bf0" :answer-field/id #uuid "72cb9bf0-ccb6-559e-9563-0a1c32c36bf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f1d57981-7421-525b-8f41-9898dd97c53f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9e96a966-e103-5eca-8449-1e546e92f65c" "answer-e28ba213-5c51-599b-8ddb-1afbff9590c0" "answer-f1d57981-7421-525b-8f41-9898dd97c53f" "answer-5d67ca65-ba00-5824-8928-96d114742288" "answer-1fba4aa5-0f41-512c-aee9-ad567870f839"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e96a966-e103-5eca-8449-1e546e92f65c" :answer/id #uuid "9e96a966-e103-5eca-8449-1e546e92f65c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\,375" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e28ba213-5c51-599b-8ddb-1afbff9590c0" :answ